\f0\fs28\b Use-Case Flow Specification\b0\fs22\line
\fs24\b Use Case: File Lost Baggage Claim\b0\fs22\line
Primary Actor: Passenger\line
Supporting Actors: System (automated matching engine), Baggage Service Agent (receives the resulting claim)\line\line

\b Preconditions\b0\line
1. The passenger's flight has landed and the bag was not retrieved at the carousel.\line
2. The passenger is authenticated in the airline app/portal or is using an airport self-service kiosk.\line\line

\b Postconditions (on success)\b0\line
1. A claim record exists with a unique Claim ID and status "Investigating."\line
2. The claim has automatically been checked against terminal scan logs (via the "Match Claim to Scan Logs" use case).\line
3. The passenger has received a confirmation notification containing the Claim ID.\line\line

\b Main Success Scenario\b0\line
1. Passenger selects "Report Lost Baggage" in the app or kiosk.\line
2. System displays a claim form requesting flight number, bag tag ID (if known), bag description, and last known checkpoint.\line
3. Passenger enters the required details and submits the form.\line
4. System validates the input (required fields present, flight number format valid).\line
5. System generates a unique Claim ID and creates a claim record with status "Investigating."\line
6. System automatically triggers \i Match Claim to Scan Logs\i0  («include») against all connected terminal scan logs.\line
7. System sends the passenger a confirmation notification with the Claim ID via \i Notify Passenger\i0  («include»).\line
8. Use case ends successfully.\line\line

\b Alternate Flow \endash  4a. Invalid or Missing Input\b0\line
4a1. System detects a missing required field or an invalid flight number format.\line
4a2. System displays an inline error message identifying the offending field.\line
4a3. Passenger corrects the input and resubmits (returns to step 3).\line
4a4. If the passenger abandons the form without resubmitting, no claim is created and the use case ends without success.\line
}
